  "journal": [
    {
      "id": "j.makers.exact.jiahu",
      "claimKind": "observation",
      "text": "The Henan Museum identifies this featured flute as 23.1 cm long, with seven holes along a crane-family wing bone. It was excavated at Jiahu, Wuyang, Henan, in 1987. The museum’s period range is an archaeological attribution. The practice tones use a fictional model and give no tuning measurements or fingering for the collection object."
    },
    {
      "id": "j.makers.exact.disk",
      "claimKind": "observation",
      "text": "The Penn Museum dates this limestone or calcite disk from Ur to about 2300 BCE. It records a relief on one side and an inscription on the other naming Enheduanna and identifying her as the daughter of Sargon. The inscription identifies Enheduanna. The disk alone gives no surviving manuscript in her hand and no proof of who composed a particular hymn."
    },
    {
      "id": "j.makers.exact.laguna",
      "claimKind": "observation",
      "text": "The National Museum records this inscribed copperplate from the Pagsanjan River Delta, Laguna, with the date 900 CE. In Postma’s preliminary reading, a document clears a debt associated with Namwran. Angkatan, Bukah and the witness Kasumuran appear in the reading. The preliminary reading names a person, a clearance and witnesses. It leaves their individual voices and any later otherwise unrecorded debt open."
    }
  ]
